B.aiP=new A.b8(B.aBG,B.aJO,"The tashahhud is said sitting in the second rak'ah of prayers of three or four rak'ahs (the short form), and in the last sitting of every prayer. The Prophet \ufdfa taught Ibn Mas'ud its words (Sahih al-Bukhari 831):\n\n\u0627\u0644\u062a\u0651\u064e\u062d\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u0650\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0635\u0651\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0648\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0637\u0651\u064e\u064a\u0651\u0650\u0628\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f\u060c \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0651\u064e\u0644\u0627\u064e\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0643\u064e \u0623\u064e\u064a\u0651\u064f\u0647\u064e\u0627 \u0627\u0644\u0646\u0651\u064e\u0628\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064f \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u062d\u0652\u0645\u064e\u0629\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u0650 \u0648\u064e\u0628\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0643\u064e\u0627\u062a\u064f\u0647\u064f\u060c \u0627\u0644\u0633\u0651\u064e\u0644\u0627\u064e\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0646\u064e\u0627 \u0648\u064e\u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0639\u0650\u0628\u064e\u0627\u062f\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0635\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0650\u062d\u0650\u064a\u0646\u064e\u200f \u0623\u064e\u0634\u0652\u0647\u064e\u062f\u064f \u0623\u064e\u0646\u0652 \u0644\u0627\u064e \u0625\u0650\u0644\u064e\u0647\u064e \u0625\u0650\u0644\u0627\u0651\u064e \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u064f\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0623\u064e\u0634\u0652\u0647\u064e\u062f\u064f \u0623\u064e\u0646\u0651\u064e \u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064b\u0627 \u0639\u064e\u0628\u0652\u062f\u064f\u0647\u064f \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0633\u064f\u0648\u0644\u064f\u0647\u064f\n\nAt-tahiyyatu lillahi was-salawatu wat-tayyibat. As-salamu 'alayka ayyuhan-nabiyyu wa rahmatullahi wa barakatuh. As-salamu 'alayna wa 'ala 'ibadillahis-salihin. Ash-hadu an la ilaha illallah, wa ash-hadu anna Muhammadan 'abduhu wa rasuluh.\n\nMeaning: all greetings, prayers and good things are for Allah. Peace be upon you, O Prophet, and the mercy of Allah and His blessings. Peace be upon us and upon the righteous servants of Allah. I bear witness that there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness that Muhammad is His servant and messenger.\n\nIn the last sitting, after the tashahhud, you send blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa. The Prophet \ufdfa taught his companions to say (Sahih al-Bukhari 3370):\n\n\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u064f\u0645\u0651\u064e \u0635\u064e\u0644\u0651\u0650 \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064d\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0622\u0644\u0650 \u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064d\u060c \u0643\u064e\u0645\u064e\u0627 \u0635\u064e\u0644\u0651\u064e\u064a\u0652\u062a\u064e \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0625\u0650\u0628\u0652\u0631\u064e\u0627\u0647\u0650\u064a\u0645\u064e \u0648\u064e\u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0622\u0644\u0650 \u0625\u0650\u0628\u0652\u0631\u064e\u0627\u0647\u0650\u064a\u0645\u064e\u060c \u0625\u0650\u0646\u0651\u064e\u0643\u064e \u062d\u064e\u0645\u0650\u064a\u062f\u064c \u0645\u064e\u062c\u0650\u064a\u062f\u064c \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u064f\u0645\u0651\u064e \u0628\u064e\u0627\u0631\u0650\u0643\u0652 \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064d\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0622\u0644\u0650 \u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064d\u060c \u0643\u064e\u0645\u064e\u0627 \u0628\u064e\u0627\u0631\u064e\u0643\u0652\u062a\u064e \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0625\u0650\u0628\u0652\u0631\u064e\u0627\u0647\u0650\u064a\u0645\u064e\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0649 \u0622\u0644\u0650 \u0625\u0650\u0628\u0652\u0631\u064e\u0627\u0647\u0650\u064a\u0645\u064e\u060c \u0625\u0650\u0646\u0651\u064e\u0643\u064e \u062d\u064e\u0645\u0650\u064a\u062f\u064c \u0645\u064e\u062c\u0650\u064a\u062f\u064c\n\nAllahumma salli 'ala Muhammadin wa 'ala ali Muhammad, kama sallayta 'ala Ibrahima wa 'ala ali Ibrahim, innaka hamidun majid. Allahumma barik 'ala Muhammadin wa 'ala ali Muhammad, kama barakta 'ala Ibrahima wa 'ala ali Ibrahim, innaka hamidun majid.\n\nThen you may ask Allah for whatever you wish before the salam. In the second sitting of a three- or four-rak'ah prayer, the schools differ on whether to stop at the tashahhud or add the blessings: ask your imam.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 831, 3370.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]",!0,"")
B.aFs=s(["\\bsujud (?:as |al |of )?sahw\\b","\\bsujud sahu\\b","\\bsajdah sahw\\b","\\bforgetfulness prostration\\b","\\bprostration of forgetfulness\\b","\\bforgot\\b.*\\b(?:rakah|tashahhud|sujud|ruku|sit)\\b","\\bforget\\b.*\\b(?:rakah|tashahhud)\\b","\\b(?:lost count|not sure|unsure|doubt)\\b.*\\brakah\\b","\\bmistake (?:in|during) (?:the )?prayers?\\b","\\badded (?:an )?extra rakah\\b","\\bprayed (?:an )?extra\\b","\\bsahw\\b","\\bmade a mistake\\b.*\\bprayers?\\b","\\bcounted wrong\\b","\\bhow (?:do|to) (?:i )?fix.*mistake.*prayer\\b"],t.s)
B.ahs=new A.b8(B.aFs,B.c,"Sujud as-sahw (the prostration of forgetfulness) is two extra prostrations you make when you forget something in the prayer: you add a rak'ah or movement, leave out a required act (like the middle sitting), or are unsure how many rak'ahs you prayed. After them you sit and give the salam.\n\nWhen you make it differs between the schools:\n- Hanafi: after the salam (one salam, then the two prostrations, then tashahhud and the final salam).\n- Shafi'i: before the final salam.\n- Maliki: before the salam when something was left out, after it when something was added.\n- Hanbali: follows the hadiths, so before the salam in most cases, after it where the Prophet \ufdfa did so.\n\nBoth ways are in the Sunnah. When the Prophet \ufdfa got up from the middle sitting without sitting, he made two prostrations before the salam (Sahih al-Bukhari 829). When he gave the salam after two rak'ahs by mistake, then completed the prayer, he made two prostrations after the salam (Sahih al-Bukhari 1229).\n\nIf you are unsure how many rak'ahs you prayed, the Prophet \ufdfa said to put the doubt aside, build on what you are sure of and make two prostrations (Sahih Muslim 571). The sujud does not repair a pillar you left out; that must be prayed again.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 829, 1229; Sahih Muslim 571.",!0,"")
B.b47=s(["\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)|can i|should i) (?:pray|perform (?:the )?prayers?|do (?:the )?prayers?|offer (?:the )?prayers?|make (?:the )?prayers?)\\b$","\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)|can i|should i) pray (?:salah|prayer|properly|correctly|step by step)?$","\\bhow to pray\\b.*\\b(?:step by step|for beginners|for a beginner|for new muslims?|properly|correctly|the right way|in islam)\\b","\\bteach me (?:how )?to pray\\b","\\bsteps? (?:of|for|to) (?:the )?prayers?\\b","\\bprayers? steps?\\b","\\bi (?:want|need) to learn (?:how )?to pray\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do i) start praying\\b","\\bbeginner.*prayers?\\b","\\bhow to perform salah\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|are) the (?:steps|actions) (?:of|in) (?:the )?prayers?\\b","\\bhows? (?:do|to) (?:i|we) pray\\b","\\b(?:learn|learning) (?:how )?to pray\\b","\\bpray(?:ing)? (?:as|for) an? (?:absolute )?beginner\\b","\\bnew to (?:praying|prayer|salah|namaz)\\b","\\b(?:fajr|dhuhr|zuhr|asr|maghrib|isha) (?:prayer |salah |namaz )?step by step\\b","\\bstep by step\\b.*\\b(?:fajr|dhuhr|zuhr|asr|maghrib|isha|salah|namaz|prayer)\\b","\\bnamaz (?:kaise|ka tarika)\\b","\\bnamaz parhne ka tarika\\b"],t.s)
B.aFY=s(["\\b(?:at night|night prayers?|qiyam|qiyaam|janazah|funeral|eid|jumuah|friday|witr|taraweeh|tahajjud|istikhara|travel\\w*|sick|sitting|dhuhr|asr|maghrib|isha|women|woman|child|kid)\\b"],t.s)
B.ahC=new A.b8(B.b47,B.aFY,'Here is a two-rak\'ah prayer from start to finish. Be in wudu, face the qibla, and stand where you will pray.\n1. Stand and make the intention in your heart. Say "Allahu akbar" (raise your hands to the ears or shoulders) and fold your hands over your chest or navel (the schools differ), right over left.\n2. Recite the opening du\'a (optional in some schools), say "A\'udhu billahi minash-shaytanir-rajim", then "Bismillah" and al-Fatihah, and say "Amin". Then recite another surah or a few verses, such as Surah al-Ikhlas.\n3. Say "Allahu akbar" and bow (ruku\'): back straight, hands on knees. Say "Subhana Rabbiyal-\'Azim" (Glory to my Lord, the Mighty) three times.\n4. Rise saying "Sami\'allahu liman hamidah" (Allah hears whoever praises Him), then "Rabbana wa lakal-hamd" (Our Lord, to You is the praise). Stand straight.\n5. Say "Allahu akbar" and go down to prostration (sujud): forehead, nose, both palms, both knees and the toes of both feet on the ground. Say "Subhana Rabbiyal-A\'la" (Glory to my Lord, the Most High) three times.\n6. Say "Allahu akbar", sit up briefly, then prostrate a second time with the same words.\n7. Stand up for the second rak\'ah and repeat from step 2 (without the opening du\'a).\n8. After the second sujud of the last rak\'ah, sit and say the tashahhud, then the blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa.\n9. Give the salam, turning your head right then left: "As-salamu \'alaykum wa rahmatullah".\n\nThree- and four-rak\'ah prayers: sit for the tashahhud after the second rak\'ah, then stand for the rest and sit again at the end. Fard Fajr is 2, Dhuhr 4, Asr 4, Maghrib 3, Isha 4.\n\nThe How to Pray guide in this app shows each step in order. If you are learning, pray at your own pace and ask your imam to watch and correct you.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-prayer]',!0,"")
B.aQ0=s(["\\b(?:touch|touching|hold|holding|carry|carrying|read|reading|recite|reciting|open|opening)\\b.*\\b(?:quran|mushaf)\\b.*\\b(?:without|with no|wudu|ablution|impure|janabah|period|menstruat\\w*|unclean)\\b","\\b(?:quran|mushaf)\\b.*\\b(?:without wudu|without ablution|not in wudu|without being pure|while impure|in janabah)\\b","\\bwudu\\b.*\\b(?:touch|hold|read|recite|carry)\\w*\\b.*\\b(?:quran|mushaf)\\b","\\b(?:quran|mushaf)\\b.*\\bwudu\\b","\\b(?:quran|mushaf)\\b.*\\b(?:phone|screen|app|tablet|digital|ebook|online)\\b.*\\b(?:wudu|touch|impure|bathroom|toilet)\\b","\\b(?:phone|screen|app|tablet|digital)\\b.*\\bquran\\b.*\\b(?:wudu|touch|bathroom|toilet)\\b","\\bquran\\b.*\\b(?:bathroom|toilet)\\b"],t.s)
B.ah_=new A.b8(B.aQ0,B.c,"Touching the physical copy of the Qur'an (the mushaf):\n- Most scholars, including the four schools, say you should be in wudu to touch or carry the written Qur'an, based on \"None touch it except the purified\" (Qur'an 56:77-79). Many explain this verse as being about the angels in the heavenly record, and use other reports to support the ruling.\n- A minority of scholars say it is not required, as there is no clear text; many modern scholars take the cautious view and say wudu is best.\n- A person in janabah (and, in most views, a woman in her period) should not touch a mushaf directly. They may hold it with a cloth, or use a translation or a phone.\n\nReciting the Qur'an without wudu (from memory or from a screen) is allowed. A person in janabah or a menstruating woman is a school matter: the Hanafi, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools restrict reciting; the Maliki school allows a menstruating woman to recite, and so do some other scholars, since the report quoted for the ban is graded weak (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 131, da'if per Darussalam).\n\nOn a phone, tablet or computer: most contemporary scholars say a screen showing the Qur'an is not treated like a printed mushaf, so you may touch the device without wudu, but you should still treat it with respect and not take it into the toilet if you can avoid it. This is the common view, and some are stricter.\n\nWhen in doubt, make wudu first; it costs little and it is the way of respect.\n\nSource: Qur'an 56:77-79; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 131, da'if per Darussalam.\n[GO:/quran]",!0,"")
B.aUh=s(["\\bnail ?(?:polish|varnish|paint|lacquer)\\b","\\bmanicure\\b","\\bgel nails?\\b","\\bacrylic nails?\\b","\\b(?:make ?up|lipstick|foundation|sunscreen|sunblock|moisturi[sz]er|lotion|cream)\\b.*\\b(?:wudu|ghusl|prayer)\\b","\\b(?:wudu|ghusl)\\b.*\\b(?:make ?up|lipstick|foundation|sunscreen|sunblock|moisturi[sz]er|lotion|cream|henna|mehndi|tattoo|plaster|bandage|cast|sticker|glue|wax|paint)\\b","\\b(?:cast|plaster|bandage|splint|dressing)\\b.*\\b(?:wudu|ghusl|wipe)\\b","\\bhenna\\b.*\\b(?:wudu|prayer)\\b","\\bwater (?:reach|reaching|barrier)\\b"],t.s)
B.aiW=new A.b8(B.aUh,B.c,'Wudu and ghusl are valid only if water reaches the skin and hair it has to wash. Anything that forms a layer which water cannot pass through stops the wash from counting.\n\n- Nail polish, gel or acrylic nails, wax, thick paint, glue, and waterproof makeup: they block the water, so remove them before wudu. Many women pray without polish for this reason, or use a breathable "wudu-friendly" polish that lets water through (some scholars accept it).\n- Henna and dye: they stain but do not form a layer, so wudu is valid.\n- Lotion and oil on the skin: valid as long as water still touches the skin.\n- Plaster, bandage or a cast on a wound: wash the rest, and wipe wet hands over the cover (scholars allow this when removing it would harm you or delay healing). The Shafi\'i school also asks for tayammum for the covered part.\n- A tattoo under the skin: wudu is valid.\n- Rings and watches: move them so water goes beneath, or take them off.\n\nIf you are not sure that water reached, wash again. Ask your imam for your own case.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-wudu]',!0,"")
B.aMb=s(["\\b(?:obligatory|fard|farz|essential|required|mandatory|compulsory|pillars?|rukn|arkan|minimum)\\b.*\\bwudu\\b","\\bwudu\\b.*\\b(?:obligatory|fard|farz|essential|required|mandatory|compulsory|pillars?|rukn|arkan|minimum)\\b","\\bconditions? of wudu\\b","\\bwhat is (?:needed|required) (?:for|in) wudu\\b","\\bfard acts? of wudu\\b"],t.s)
B.aOb=s(["\\b(?:break|breaks|breaking|invalidat\\w*|nullif\\w*|cancel\\w*)\\b"],t.s)
B.agW=new A.b8(B.aMb,B.aOb,"The Qur'an names the obligatory acts of wudu: \"wash your faces and your forearms to the elbows and wipe over your heads and wash your feet to the ankles\" (Qur'an 5:6). So four acts are agreed:\n1. Wash the whole face (from the hairline to the chin, and ear to ear).\n2. Wash both arms up to and including the elbows.\n3. Wipe the head (the Hanafi school says about a quarter of it; the Shafi'i school says a few hairs; the Maliki and Hanbali schools say the whole head).\n4. Wash both feet up to and including the ankles.\n\nSome schools add more obligations. The Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali schools require the intention (niyyah) in the heart; the Hanafi school counts it as a sunnah. The Shafi'i and Hanbali schools also require doing the four acts in this order. The Maliki and Hanbali schools require doing them without a long break in between. Rinsing the mouth and nose is a sunnah in wudu in most views, though the Hanbali school makes it obligatory.\n\nEverything else (bismillah, washing the hands first, rinsing, washing three times, the right side first, the siwak, the du'a after) is sunnah. Washing once is enough for the obligation.\n\nTo learn the full wudu step by step, see the Wudu guide.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:6.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-wudu]",!0,"")
B.b3o=s(["\\bhow (?:to|do (?:i|we|you)|can i|should i|is) (?:make|perform|do|take|done|made|performed|taken)\\b.*\\bwudu\\b","\\bsteps? (?:of|for|to|in) (?:making |doing |performing )?wudu\\b","\\bwudu steps?\\b","\\bmethod of wudu\\b","\\bwudu (?:step by step|for beginners|procedure|method|how)\\b","\\bwhat is wudu\\b","\\blearn wudu\\b","\\bteach me wudu\\b","\\bhow to wash for prayer\\b","\\bperform (?:the )?wudu\\b","^wudu$","\\bhow to do ablution\\b","\\b(?:wuzu|wudu|wudhu|wuzoo|wazu|wudoo) (?:kaise|ka tarika|karne ka tarika|karte)\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do i|do you) (?:do |make |perform )?(?:wuzu|wudhu|wazu|wudoo|ablution)\\b","\\bablution\\b"],t.s)
