B.c_B=new A.p1("Why Marriage at All","Marriage is described in the Qur'an with four words: sukun (tranquility), mawaddah (affection), and rahmah (mercy) (30:21). Notice what is missing: passion, romance, excitement. The house runs on mercy the way the body runs on blood.",u.dG)
B.c_y=new A.p1("The Best of You","The best of you is the one who is best to his wife (Tirmidhi 3895, hasan per Darussalam, sahih per al-Albani). And the most complete believer in faith is the one with the best character (Tirmidhi 1162, hasan per Darussalam). The measure of a Muslim is not the masjid face; it is the kitchen face.",u.gq)
B.c_t=new A.p1("Mercy Between You","Allah describes the spouses as 'garments' for one another (2:187) \u2014 the closest thing to the skin, covering faults, providing warmth. The garment does not criticize the body it covers.",u.e7)
B.aVH=s([B.c_B,B.c_y,B.c_t],t.IV)
B.c_E=new A.PI("Marriage \u2014 Half the Deen","The Prophet \ufdfa said: when a servant marries, they have completed half of their religion \u2014 so let them fear Allah in the remaining half (Bayhaqi). The home is the smallest masjid.",B.aVH)
B.c_C=new A.p1("The First Teacher","The mother of every person carries them in weakness upon weakness (31:14) \u2014 and the Prophet \ufdfa was asked who most deserves good company: your mother, three times, then your father (Bukhari 5971). The home is a madrasa whether you intend it or not.",u.kD)
B.c_z=new A.p1("Teaching the Prayer","Command your children to pray at seven and discipline them about it at ten, and separate their beds (Abu Dawud 495, hasan sahih per al-Albani). Before seven the prayer is invited, not enforced \u2014 love first, law second, so the law lands softer.",u.h0)
B.c_w=new A.p1("Mercy, Not Severity","The Prophet \ufdfa said: Allah is Merciful and loves mercy in all things (Bukhari 6927). And he never struck a servant or a woman, and never struck anything with his hand except in battle (Muslim 2328). The strongest homes are the gentlest.",u.dW)
B.c_A=new A.p1("The Best Provision","The best thing a father can give his child is good manners (Tirmidhi 1952, weak per Darussalam). And the most precious inheritance is taqwa \u2014 it is the only wealth that cannot be lost, taxed, or inherited away.",u.mg)
B.aSf=s([B.c_C,B.c_z,B.c_w,B.c_A],t.IV)
B.c_D=new A.PI("Children \u2014 The Trust","Your children are an amanah \u2014 a trust. The Prophet \ufdfa said: each of you is a shepherd, and each of you is responsible for his flock (Bukhari 893).",B.aSf)
B.c_u=new A.p1("Serving the Family","Aisha was asked what the Prophet \ufdfa did at home. She said: he was in the service of his family (Bukhari 676). He mended his clothes, milked the goat, swept. The man who led the ummah led the dishes.",u.fG)
B.c_x=new A.p1("The Family That Prays","The Prophet \ufdfa said: the one who remembers his Lord and the one who does not are like the living and the dead (Bukhari 6407). Dhikr at the door, before meals, at sleep \u2014 small phrases that keep the heart alive.",u.r)
B.c_v=new A.p1("Forgiveness in the House","The Prophet \ufdfa said: Allah has mercy on the man who is lenient when he sells, when he buys, and when he asks for what is owed (Bukhari 2076). The house needs this most: forgive the harsh word before the day ends.",u.kr)
B.b4V=s([B.c_u,B.c_x,B.c_v],t.IV)
B.c_F=new A.PI("The Home as Worship","The house is not a pause between acts of worship \u2014 it is one.",B.b4V)
B.b1b=s([B.c_E,B.c_D,B.c_F],A.ac("x<PI>"))
B.bSJ=new A.jP("The Greeting Is a Qur\u2019anic Sentence","\u0627\u0644\u0633\u064e\u0644\u0627\u0645\u064f \u0639\u064e\u0644\u064e\u064a\u0643\u064f\u0645\u0652","As-salamu alaykum \u2014 peace be upon you \u2014 is a Qur\u2019anic formula: the Prophet \ufdfa is told to greet the believers who come to him with it (6:54), and the angels greet the people of Paradise with \u201cpeace be upon you for what you patiently endured\u201d (13:24). The reply, wa alaykum as-salam, mirrors it. Every time you greet a Muslim, you are speaking Qur\u2019anic Arabic.","Qur\u2019an 6:54; 13:24",null,null)
B.bSR=new A.jP("Family Words You Already Own","\u0623\u064e\u0628\u064c / \u0623\u064f\u0645\u0651\u064c / \u0627\u0628\u0652\u0646\u064c / \u0628\u0650\u0646\u0652\u062a\u064c","Ab (father), umm (mother), ibn/bint (son/daughter) \u2014 the same words that carry the Qur\u2019an\u2019s family passages: Yusuf says to his father \u201cya abati\u201d (12:4), a mother carries her child (46:15), Luqman counsels his son (31:13), and Maryam is ibnat \u2018Imran (66:12). Learn these four and the family words in many verses will already be familiar.","Qur\u2019an 12:4; 46:15; 31:13; 66:12",null,null)
B.bSM=new A.jP("The Definite Article: Al-","\u0627\u0644\u0643\u0650\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064f \u2014 \u0643\u0650\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064c",u.cS,"Grammar note",null,null)
B.bSQ=new A.jP("Allah Is the Name, Rabb Is the Word","\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u064f \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e","Allah is the proper Name; rabb is the noun (Lord, Nurturer). In the Qur\u2019an rabb is almost always joined to what it governs \u2014 rabbi al-\u2018alamin (Lord of the worlds), rabbuna (our Lord), rabbuka (your Lord) \u2014 rather than standing alone with al-. Watch how the Qur\u2019an uses each word.","Qur\u2019an 1:2","/quran","See it in Al-Fatiha")
B.aPI=s([B.bSJ,B.bSR,B.bSM,B.bSQ],t.n1)
B.bT1=new A.BE("First Words, Real Words",B.aPI)
B.bSN=new A.jP("Al-Fatiha, Word by Word","\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0652\u062f\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650 \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e",u.gO,"Qur\u2019an 1:2","/quran","Recite it in the reader")
B.bSU=new A.jP("The Formula You Say 17 Times a Day","\u0625\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0643\u064e \u0646\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062f\u064f","Iyyaka na\u2019budu \u2014 You alone we worship. Iyyaka (You) is placed before the verb; that fronting is what gives the sentence its \u201calone\u201d. Na\u2019budu is the present-tense verb, and the na- at its start means \u201cwe\u201d. Salah is a daily Arabic lesson the body never forgets.","Qur\u2019an 1:5",null,null)
B.bSV=new A.jP("Slow Is the Standard","\u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u062a\u0651\u0650\u0644\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0642\u064f\u0631\u0652\u0622\u0646\u064e \u062a\u064e\u0631\u0652\u062a\u0650\u064a\u0644\u0627\u064b","And recite the Qur\u2019an with measured recitation (73:4). Slow enough to hear each letter. Arabic learned slowly stays; Arabic rushed evaporates.","Qur\u2019an 73:4",null,null)
B.aWb=s([B.bSN,B.bSU,B.bSV],t.n1)
B.bT0=new A.BE("Reading Whole Verses",B.aWb)
B.bSL=new A.jP("The Simplest Sentences","\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u064f \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0646\u064e\u0627","Arabic needs only two words for a full sentence. Two nouns make a nominal sentence: Allahu rabbuna \u2014 Allah is our Lord (42:15), with no word for \u201cis\u201d. A verb and its doer make a verbal sentence: khalaqa Allahu \u2014 Allah created (29:44). Most sentences you will read are built from these two patterns.","Qur'an 42:15; 29:44",null,null)
B.bSW=new A.jP("The Verb: Past and Present","\u062e\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0642\u064e \u0648\u064e\u064a\u064e\u062e\u0644\u064f\u0642\u064f","Khalaqa \u2014 He created (past, 96:1). Yakhluqu \u2014 He creates (present, 28:68). The past is fixed; the present is alive. Arabic shows time in the verb's shape, not in extra words. Learn khalaqa and you have the pattern of a typical three-letter past verb.","Qur'an 96:1; 28:68",null,null)
B.bSO=new A.jP("The Feminine Marker: The Round Ta","\u0645\u064f\u0624\u0652\u0645\u0650\u0646\u064c \u0648\u064e\u0645\u064f\u0624\u0652\u0645\u0650\u0646\u064e\u0629\u064c","The round ta (\u0629) at the end of a word often marks the feminine: mu'min (a believing man) and mu'minah (a believing woman). The Qur'an uses both: \u201cit is not for a believing man or a believing woman\u201d (33:36). Note that al-Rahman and al-Rahim in the basmala are both masculine forms \u2014 the ta marker is a common sign of the feminine, not a rule without exceptions.","Qur'an 33:36",null,null)
B.bSX=new A.jP("The Broken Plural","\u0643\u064e\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064c \u2192 \u0643\u064f\u062a\u064f\u0628\u064c","Not every plural adds a sound ending. Kitab (book) becomes kutub (books) \u2014 the vowels inside change shape. This is the broken plural, and it is everywhere in the Qur'an. Learn it as a pattern to recognize, not a rule to fear.","Qur'an 2:285 (wa-kutubihi)",null,null)
B.aTI=s([B.bSL,B.bSW,B.bSO,B.bSX],t.n1)
B.bSZ=new A.BE("Building Sentences",B.aTI)
B.bSH=new A.jP("I, You, He \u2014 in the Qur'an","\u0625\u0650\u064a\u0651\u064e\u0627\u0643\u064e \u0646\u064e\u0639\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062f\u064f","Ana \u2014 I. Anta \u2014 you (masculine). Anti \u2014 you (feminine). Huwa \u2014 he. Hiya \u2014 she. In Al-Fatiha you say iyyaka na'budu \u2014 You alone we worship. The -ka is \u201cyou\u201d, and the na- on the verb is \u201cwe\u201d: pronouns can ride inside and on the end of words, invisible until you learn to see them.","Qur'an 1:5",null,null)
B.bSK=new A.jP("The Attached Pronoun: -ka and -ki","\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650\u064a \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0643\u064e \u0648\u064e\u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u064f\u0646\u064e\u0627","Rabbi (my Lord), rabbuka (your Lord, masculine), rabbuki (your Lord, feminine), rabbuna (our Lord). One word, four meanings, one small sound added. This is Arabic's economy \u2014 a single attached letter carries a whole relationship.","Qur'an 93:3 (rabbuka); 19:24 (rabbuki); 42:15 (rabbuna)",null,null)
B.bST=new A.jP("The Dual: Two of Something","\u064a\u064e\u0648\u0652\u0645\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0646\u0650","Yawm (day) becomes yawmayni (two days) \u2014 the dual ending -ayni (or -\u0101ni when the word is the subject). The Qur'an uses it precisely: the two gardens of the man in Surah 18 (18:32), and the earth made in two days in Surah 41 (41:9). Arabic counts two as its own number, not as 'two ones.'","Qur'an 18:32 (jannatayn); 41:9 (yawmayn)",null,null)
B.aQU=s([B.bSH,B.bSK,B.bST],t.n1)
B.bT_=new A.BE("The Pronouns You Pray With",B.aQU)
B.bSP=new A.jP("The Idaafa: Possession in Two Words","\u0643\u064e\u062a\u064e\u0627\u0628\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650","Kitabu Allah \u2014 the Book of Allah. Two nouns side by side, and the relationship is possession. The first word loses its 'the' (kitabu, not al-kitabu); the second keeps it (Allah, already definite). This construction is everywhere \u2014 every 'of' in translation is often an idaafa in Arabic.","Qur'an 35:29 (kitaba Allahi)",null,null)
B.bSS=new A.jP("Names Are Already Definite","\u0645\u064f\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0651\u064e\u062f\u064c \u0631\u064e\u0633\u064f\u0648\u0644\u064f \u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650","A personal name is already definite, so it never takes al-: Muhammad, never al-Muhammad. Muhammadun rasulu Allah (48:29) is a name followed by an idaafa. A few names also drop the tanwin ending \u2014 Ahmadu in 61:6 \u2014 a pattern grammarians call a diptote. For now, just notice it.","Qur'an 48:29; 61:6",null,null)
B.bSI=new A.jP("Putting It Together: One Verse, Fully","\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0645\u0652\u062f\u064f \u0644\u0650\u0644\u0651\u0647\u0650 \u0631\u064e\u0628\u0651\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u0627\u0644\u064e\u0645\u064a\u0646\u064e","You now know the parts of this verse: the definite article (al-hamdu, al-\u2018alamin), the prefix li- meaning \u201cbelongs to\u201d (li-llahi), the idaafa (rabbi al-\u2018alamin \u2014 Lord of the worlds), and the plural ending -in on al-\u2018alamin (a sound masculine plural). Read it again \u2014 you are not transliterating anymore. You are reading.","Qur'an 1:2","/quran","Read it in the reader")
B.aOO=s([B.bSP,B.bSS,B.bSI],t.n1)
B.bSY=new A.BE("Reading With Possession",B.aOO)
